    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Zoom better than Google Meet?</h3>
    <p>For large meetings, webinars, and enterprise use, Zoom is better. It supports up to 1,000 participants on higher plans, has more advanced controls, and includes Zoom Whiteboard and Webinars. For quick team meetings and Google Workspace users, Google Meet is better because it is integrated with Gmail and Calendar, requires no software install, and is included with Google Workspace plans.</p>

    <h3>Is Google Meet really free?</h3>
    <p>Google Meet has a free tier that allows meetings up to 60 minutes with up to 100 participants. The free tier includes screen sharing, captions, and noise cancellation. Paid features come with Google Workspace plans, starting at $7.99 per user per month (Business Starter), which extends meetings to 24 hours and adds recording, hand raising, and polling. Zoom's free tier is more limited at 40 minutes.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for large meetings and webinars?</h3>
    <p>Zoom is better for large meetings and webinars. Zoom Business supports up to 300 participants, Zoom Enterprise supports up to 1,000, and Zoom Webinars supports up to 100,000 attendees. Google Meet supports up to 100 participants on free, 150 on Business plans, and 1,000 on Enterprise Plus. For webinars specifically, Zoom Webinars is a dedicated product with registration pages, Q&A, and analytics that Google Meet does not match.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Zoom and Google Meet together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, many organizations use both. A common setup is Google Meet for internal team meetings (because it launches from Calendar with one click) and Zoom for external meetings with clients, webinars, and large events. Both tools can coexist without conflict. Some meeting platforms like Zoom Rooms can join Google Meet calls, though the experience is not seamless.</p>

    <h3>Which has better AI features?</h3>
    <p>Zoom AI Companion is included at no extra cost with paid Zoom plans and provides meeting summaries, action items, chat summaries, and whiteboard generation. Google Meet offers Gemini meeting summaries through Google AI One Premium at $19.99 per month, which is a separate subscription. For AI features included in the base price, Zoom wins. For deeper AI integration across documents and email, Google's Gemini bundle wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is more secure?</h3>
    <p>Both are highly secure. Zoom uses AES-256 GCM encryption, supports end-to-end encryption for one-on-one calls, and is SOC 2 Type II and HIPAA compliant. Google Meet uses the same encryption as Google Workspace, is SOC 2 Type II and HIPAA compliant, and benefits from Google's broader security infrastructure. For enterprise compliance, both meet the bar. For end-to-end encryption, Zoom offers it for one-on-one calls while Google Meet does not.</p>

